## 4. Component Communication

### `props` (The Care Package)

- **In Plain English:** Data sent **downwards** from a parent component to a child component. The child can read this data, but it is **read-only**—the child is not allowed to change it directly.
- **Code Example (Child Component):**

  ```html
  <script setup lang="ts">
    // Macro used to define incoming data structure
    defineProps<{
      username: string;
      age?: number; // Optional prop
    }>();
  </script>

  <template>
    <p>User: {{ username }} (Age: {{ age ?? 'N/A' }})</p>
  </template>
  ```

### `emit` (The Walkie-Talkie)

- **In Plain English:** A message sent **upwards** from a child component to its parent. Because a child cannot change a prop, it must "shout" an event up to the parent, telling the parent to update the data instead.
- **Code Example (Child Component):**

  ```html
  <script setup lang="ts">
    // Define the events this child is allowed to shout
    const emit = defineEmits<{
      (e: "deleteUser", id: number): void;
    }>();

    const handleClick = () => {
      emit("deleteUser", 42); // Send event name and data up
    };
  </script>

  <template>
    <button @click="handleClick">Delete Me</button>
  </template>
  ```

### `provide` & `inject` (Long Distance Broadcaster)

Used to share data across many component layers without manually passing props down through middle components ("prop drilling").

#### `provide` (The Broadcaster)

- **In Plain English:** A top-level component sets up a data stream and broadcasts it under a specific key name.
- **Code Example (Grandparent Component):**

  ```html
  <script setup lang="ts">
    import { provide, ref, type InjectionKey } from "vue";

    // 1. Create a typed key
    export const themeKey = Symbol() as InjectionKey<string>;
    const currentTheme = ref<string>("dark-mode");

    // 2. Broadcast the data
    provide(themeKey, currentTheme.value);
  </script>
  ```

#### `inject` (The Radio Receiver)

- **In Plain English:** A deeply nested child component tunes into the broadcast key set up by an ancestor and immediately gains access to that data.
- **Code Example (Deep Child Component):**

  ```html
  <script setup lang="ts">
    import { inject } from "vue";
    import { themeKey } from "./Grandparent.vue"; // Import the key

    // Tune into the broadcast. Provide a default fallback value just in case.
    const appTheme = inject(themeKey, "light-mode");
  </script>

  <template>
    <div :class="appTheme">Active Theme: {{ appTheme }}</div>
  </template>
  ```

### Comparison Table

| Feature       | Direction       | Best For                                           | TypeScript Advantage                                        |
| :------------ | :-------------- | :------------------------------------------------- | :---------------------------------------------------------- |
| **`props`**   | Downward        | Passing settings/data to immediate children.       | Enforces rigid data types via `defineProps<{}>`.            |
| **`emit`**    | Upward          | Telling the immediate parent an action happened.   | Strongly types the event names and payload contents.        |
| **`provide`** | Downward (Deep) | Broadcasting data to a whole tree of components.   | Requires an `InjectionKey` to guarantee type safety.        |
| **`inject`**  | Upward (Lookup) | Grabbing broadcasted data from a distant ancestor. | Automatically knows the variable type via the imported key. |

